                <h2>When to Use This SOP</h2>
<p>When your team operates 5 or more ad accounts simultaneously, the lack of standardization in budget allocation, creative reuse, recharge timing, and reconciliation can create chaos. This article provides a ready-to-implement SOP for multi-account management.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Define Account Classification Standards</h2>
<p>Account classification is the foundation. We recommend classifying accounts based on two dimensions: <strong>投放目的</strong> (campaign objective) and <strong>历史表现</strong> (historical performance).</p>
<h3>Classification by Campaign Objective</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Account Type</th>
<th>Typical Use Case</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>A</td>
<td>Core Growth Account</td>
<td>Primary product lines, key markets—balancing scale and ROI</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>B</td>
<td>Testing &amp; Exploration Account</td>
<td>New market validation, new product tests—losses acceptable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>C</td>
<td>Brand Awareness Account</td>
<td>Brand exposure, campaign drives—CPM as primary KPI</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>D</td>
<td>Supplementary Channel Account</td>
<td>Niche platforms or channels—smaller budgets</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Dynamic Adjustment by Performance</h3>
<p>Monthly scoring based on:</p>
<ul>
<li>ROAS (Return on Ad Spend)</li>
<li>CPA (Cost Per Acquisition)</li>
<li>Spend growth rate</li>
<li>Creative update frequency</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Score results determine next-month budget allocation.</strong> For example, if an A-level account's ROAS drops more than 20% for two consecutive months, it automatically downgrades to B-level, with 30% of its budget released to new testing accounts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Clarify Division of Responsibilities and Access Control</h2>
<h3>Recommended Role Allocation</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Role</th>
<th>Primary Responsibilities</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Account Admin</td>
<td>Account setup, recharge, access allocation, balance monitoring</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Media Buyer</td>
<td>Creative preparation, campaign setup, A/B testing, performance analysis</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Finance Reconciler</td>
<td>Monthly reconciliation, spend data aggregation, cost analysis</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Risk Controller</td>
<td>Anomaly monitoring, policy violation alerts, compliance review</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: For major platforms like Meta, Google, and TikTok, ensure at least two media buyers can cross-access each account to prevent account lockout due to personnel changes.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Access Control Principles</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recharge operations require dual confirmation to prevent errors or fund risks</li>
<li>Creative upload permissions can be delegated to media buyers; pause/delete permissions reserved for account admins</li>
<li>Financial reports accessible only to finance and risk control roles</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Establish Daily Operations Checklist</h2>
<h3>Daily Execution (SOP-Daily)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Balance Check</strong>: Log into each platform backend to confirm A and B accounts have sufficient balance; trigger alerts when C and D account balances fall below threshold</li>
<li><strong>Spend Monitoring</strong>: Compare yesterday's actual spend vs. planned spend; investigate if deviation exceeds ±15%</li>
<li><strong>Creative Status</strong>: Check ad review status; immediately replace any disapproved creatives</li>
</ol>
<h3>Weekly Execution (SOP-Weekly)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Account Performance Review</strong>: Compile key metrics (ROAS, CPA, spend volume) for each account; generate weekly report</li>
<li><strong>Budget Adjustment</strong>: Adjust next-week budget allocation based on previous week's performance; prioritize A-level accounts, dynamically adjust B and C levels</li>
<li><strong>Creative Iteration</strong>: Upload at least 2-3 new creative sets for testing per account</li>
</ol>
<h3>Monthly Execution (SOP-Monthly)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Account Classification Review</strong>: Re-evaluate account levels based on monthly data; adjust responsibilities and budgets accordingly</li>
<li><strong>Reconciliation</strong>: Cross-check platform statements with internal records to ensure recharge amount, spend amount, and balance are consistent</li>
<li><strong>Risk Audit</strong>: Check for abnormal IP logins, non-compliant creatives, or overspend issues</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Common Issues and Solutions</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What if a B-level testing account consistently underperforms?</strong></p>
<p>Set a 60-day observation period. If CPA remains 2x above target throughout the testing phase with no clear optimization path, consider shutting it down and reallocating budget to other promising tests.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How to avoid duplicate creative delivery across accounts?</strong></p>
<p>Build a creative library tracker documenting which creatives have run on which accounts, dates, and performance data. Search the tracker before launching to prevent internal competition from same creatives in the same market.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>The core goal of multi-account management is <strong>aligning resource investment with campaign objectives</strong>. Through the three-step approach—classification standards, clear responsibilities, and execution checklists—your team can significantly reduce management overhead and focus more energy on optimizing performance. We recommend piloting this SOP on 1-2 accounts first, then rolling out across the board once validated.</p>
